/**
 * \page core_guide Core Infrastructure
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * The shared substrate every container and algorithm is built on. These modules
 * live directly in `worse::core` (a few helpers nest under `worse::core::container`)
 * and deliberately avoid the standard library so the import surface stays small and
 * free of `std` associated-namespace overloads pulled in via ADL.
 *
 * \section core_types Fixed-width types — `worse.core.basic_type`
 *
 * The vocabulary integer/float aliases used throughout: `u8`/`u16`/`u32`/`u64`,
 * `i8`/`i16`/`i32`/`i64`, `usize`/`isize`, `f32`/`f64`. Sizes are nailed down so
 * layout and overflow behavior are explicit (e.g. the hash engines' `u16` DIB).
 *
 * \section core_utility Value categories & vocabulary — `worse.core.utility`
 *
 * Hand-rolled one-line casts — `move`, `forward`, `swap`, `exchange`,
 * `moveIfNoexcept` (returns `T const&` to force a copy when a move could throw and a
 * copy exists, preserving the strong guarantee in grow loops) — plus the small
 * vocabulary types `Pair` / `CompressedPair` (EBO) and the default comparison
 * functors `Less` / `Greater` / `EqualTo`, including the transparent `Less<>`
 * specialization that the ordered/flat containers default to.
 *
 * \warning Because these mirror `std::move`/`std::swap` etc. and the engine uses a
 * flat namespace, internal calls are written **fully qualified** (`worse::core::move`)
 * to avoid an ADL clash with `std::` overloads.
 *
 * \section core_traits Type traits & concepts — `worse.core.type_traits`
 *
 * The trait layer: `RemoveReference`, `Conditional`, `EnableIf`, the `IsXxx`
 * predicates, and — load-bearing for the contiguous containers — the in-house
 * `IsTriviallyRelocatable` (opt in with `WE_DECLARE_TRIVIALLY_RELOCATABLE`), which
 * lets `Array`/`FixedArray` grow with a single `memmove` instead of move + destroy.
 * Also the container-facing concepts (`HashFor`, `CompareFor`, `PredicateFor`).
 *
 * \section core_memory Allocation model — `worse.core.memory`, `container.allocator`
 *
 * The predictable OOM contract lives here (R45/R46): `memory::allocate` uses
 * **nothrow** `::operator new`, so the `nullptr` checks every container already has
 * become live, and `handleAllocationFailure` is a deterministic `std::abort`
 * (`[[noreturn]]`), never `unreachable()`/UB. `Allocator` is the default stateless
 * allocator (force-inlined to bare `operator new`/`delete` on the hot path, R43);
 * `AllocatorTraits` is the uniform seam containers go through.
 *
 * \section core_iterator Iterators — `worse.core.container.iterator`
 *
 * Iterator category tags and concepts (`InputIterator` … `RandomAccessIterator`),
 * `IteratorTraits`, `ReverseIterator`, the `distance`/`advance`/`next`/`prev`
 * helpers, and `addressOf` (wraps `__builtin_addressof`).
 *
 * \section core_memutil Raw-storage construction — `worse.core.container.memory_util`
 *
 * The allocator-aware uninitialized helpers the node/contiguous containers build on:
 * `uninitializedCopy`/`Move`/`Fill`/`DefaultConstruct`/`ValueConstruct`,
 * `destroyAt`/`destroyRange`, and `relocate`/`relocateBackward` (construct-then-
 * destroy, with the trivially-relocatable `memmove` shortcut).
 *
 * \section core_intrinsics Typed intrinsics — `worse.core.intrinsics`
 *
 * Thin force-inlined wrappers so hot paths read as intent, not `__builtin_*`:
 * `memCopy`/`memMove`/`memSet`, `isConstantEvaluated`, and `countTrailingZeros64`
 * (precondition `x != 0`). See \ref ctr_hash for where the SWAR `countTrailingZeros64`
 * drives SwissTable group scanning.
 *
 * \note See the \ref core_infra "Core infrastructure" topic group for the full API
 *       reference of these modules.
 */
